# Intent: the robot's own tailnet client (owner 2026-11-14: "register it to a tailscale network and then just give it any WiFi"). The unit joins the household's Tailscale network itself (MicroLink, a downloaded, pinned MIT client; recipe.sh fetches it into the build root, never into this tree) after Wi-Fi gets an address, and reaches the hub at its tailnet address from any Wi-Fi with no relay and no public address. Compiled only with CONFIG_STACKCHAN_TAILNET (recipe --tailnet); off at run time until the host calls self.tailnet.set; a boot-loop guard switches it off after 3 crashed boots in a row. Node identity lives in MicroLink's own NVS namespaces (microlink, ml_peers), which collide with none of ours.
# Check after a flash: self.tailnet.status answers {compiled,enabled,state,ip,peer,ready,internal_free,largest_block}; with a private first-enrolment build and the switch on, the serial log shows "tailnet state: connected" and the unit appears in the Tailscale machine list as maipai-robot; internal_free stays at or above 25 KB with voice, avatar and the client all running.
